{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/447330-reading-material-request-guides-to-two-different-world-world-religions","id":447330,"title":"Reading Material Request - Guides to two different world World Religions.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-03-18T00:47:45-07:00","id":447330,"title":"Reading Material Request - Guides to two different world World Religions.","content":"<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"> Hello everyone,<br /> <br /> So I was wondering if you wonderful people on the forums might help me out. Plenty of you seem clued up of folklore, mythology and religion. <br /> <br /> Being a former sociology and folklore student I'm fascinated by a lot of what human beings believe. While I've studied several different folk lore narratives in detail during my student years I've never really looked into the basic social structures that exist in several of the major organized world religions. <br /> <br /> To clarify a bit. I was brought up a Jehovahs Witness, got out of that when I was 14. Living in the UK I have a pretty good grasp of the Anglican take on Protistant Christian. I studied Judaism for about 2 years in secondary/high school. During my time as a student I took a passing interest in Buddhism and did some basic readings, nothing that in-depth. Last couple of years I've looked into Taoism. <br /> </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">I found Buddhism a bit hard to grasp, certainly I need to reread some sources to get to grips with it. I own several books on Taoism but its been a few years since I read them and I think I need a refresher course to really get to grips with the material again. However, that's neither here nor there in regards to the topic I need your help with. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\"> I'm wondering if anyone can point me towards good books for a basic beginners level (sorta &quot;idiots guide to&quot; or &quot;X for Dummies&quot; thing) understanding of Hinduism? <br /> <br /> Something with a historical account of the religions formation as well as its metaphysics, belief structures and narratives would be great. Though purely an explanitory source dealing with the internal mechanism of the religion would be fine too. <br /> <br /> The other religion I'm kinda interested in at the moment is Islam. <br /> <br /> Mainly because I want to read about it and make my own mind up about a lot of the Islamic centric issues in the world these days. Wierdly even living with a Muslism for two years during my student years did nothing to really enlighten me on Islam. Despite a fair few conversations about theology/belief narratives between us. <br /> <br /> Once again, if anyone can point me towards good beginners level literature in regards to Islam I'd be most appreciative. Same sorta content as I mentioned above for possible Hinduism sources. <br /> <br /> Obvioulsy, holy texts themselves are useful, but I'd rather have an expanitory source to pull from first before diving directly into the source material. <br /> <br /> Cheers,<br /> <br /> Hawthorn<br /> </span></span><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#0000cd\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>&quot;There is no God in the Badlands.&quot; </i></span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">- Sunny</span></span><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img41/8167/obcp.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/599-hawthorn","name":"Hawthorn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar599_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-18T01:20:47-07:00","id":447372,"title":"","content":"The one thing I'd keep in mind about both of these is that they're incredibly diverse religions. And introduction on them is going to be like an introduction to Christianity. It simply can't hit the vast breadth of variations that are out there.<br /> <br /> I don't have any good recommendations personally.<br /> <br /> Second hand I've heard:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Muslims-Religious-Beliefs-Practices-Library/dp/0415489407\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Muslims-Religious-Beliefs-Practices-Library/dp/0415489407\" target=\"_blank\"> Amazon.com </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Muslims-Religious-Beliefs-Practices-Library/dp/0415489407\" target=\"_blank\"> http://www.amazon.com/Muslims-Religious-Beliefs-Practices-Library/dp/0415489407 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Is a decent primer on Islam. Though again, second hand recommendation. So take it with a grain of salt.<br /> <br /> In doing a quick search about this while I was trying to find that, I've noticed there seems to be a mixed blessing of a lot of people putting out introductory Islam studies books right now. But it seems like it's getting hard to sort out which are the more solid scholarly works and which are getting a bit biased (in both directions) from the growing international interest on Islam due to recent history.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-18T06:57:51-07:00","id":447645,"title":"","content":"Believe it or not, <a href=\"http://www.barnesandnoble.com/w/the-complete-idiots-guide-to-hinduism-2nd-edition-linda-johnsen/1014649869?ean=9781592579051\" target=\"_blank\">The Complete Idiot's Guide to Hinduism</a> exists and is actually a pretty decent primer on the whole thing. I would also recommend listening to various lectures by <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Campbell\" target=\"_blank\">Joseph Campbell</a> as he uses a significant amount of Hindu concepts and explains them rather well.<br /> <br /> And I saw you mention getting back into Taoism, my personal favorite. Best refresher/starter I've ever found is<a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/The-Tao-Pooh-Benjamin-Hoff/dp/0140067477\" target=\"_blank\"> The Tao of Pooh</a> and <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/The-Te-Piglet-Benjamin-Hoff/dp/0140230165/ref=pd_sim_b_1?ie=UTF8&amp;refRID=164KK1SB28EF389W5XWY\" target=\"_blank\">The Te of Piglet</a>. First one teaches the concepts, second one explains how to put them into practice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}